FAERS and AERS
--------------
FAERS and legacy AERS data are published by the U.S. Food and Drug
Administration and are in the public domain. They are not included in this
repository; the open-altio command downloads them from FDA. FDA has not
reviewed or endorsed Open Altio. Reports in FAERS do not establish that a
product caused an event.


DiAna (University of Bologna)
-----------------------------
Open Altio's drug-name step uses the DiAna dictionary, and its cleaning steps
were compared, one by one, with DiAna's.

- DiAna's code: MIT License, Copyright (c) 2023 Michele Fusaroli and the DiAna
  authors. https://github.com/fusarolimichele/DiAna_cleaning and
  https://github.com/fusarolimichele/DiAna_package
- The DiAna dictionary: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
  (CC BY 4.0), published on OSF at https://osf.io/zqu89/. It is not included
  in this repository: the release build reads the file by its checksum
  (rules/drug-names.md), and our corrections to it are in
  mappings/faers/drug_names/.
- DiAna's route table (route_st.csv), the seed of mappings/faers/route/:
  CC BY 4.0, published in the same OSF project.

Cite as:
  Fusaroli M, Giunchi V, Battini V, et al. Enhancing Transparency in Defining
  Studied Drugs: The Open-Source Living DiAna Dictionary for Standardizing Drug
  Names in the FAERS. Drug Safety. 2024;47:271-284.
  doi:10.1007/s40264-023-01391-4
  Fusaroli M, Giunchi V. DiAna: Repository for FAERS cleaning and analysis.
  OSF. 2023. doi:10.17605/OSF.IO/ZQU89


Erasmus MC: faersdbstats (AEOLUS) and aioli
-------------------------------------------
Open Altio's first duplicate rule follows AEOLUS, its legacy country names were
checked against the country table faersdbstats ships, and its cleaning steps
were compared with those of faersdbstats and the aioli drug standardiser. Both
are licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

- faersdbstats: https://github.com/mi-erasmusmc/faersdbstats
- aioli: https://github.com/mi-erasmusmc/aioli

AEOLUS: Banda JM, Evans L, Vanguri RS, Tatonetti NP, Ryan PB, Shah NH.
  A curated and standardized adverse drug event resource to accelerate drug
  safety research. Scientific Data. 2016;3:160026. doi:10.1038/sdata.2016.26


ICH E2B route codes
-------------------
The route codes and names in mappings/faers/route/ are those of ICH's E2B
route of administration list (E2B(R3) code list 14, the E2B(R2) routes),
published by ICH in the E2B(R3) implementation package.


WHO ATC classification
----------------------
The drug-to-class table in mappings/ (ingredient, ATC code, WHO name, level
and index year) is derived from WHO's ATC index. The ATC codes and names are
WHO's. WHO's own index file is not included in this repository.

Cite as:
  WHO Collaborating Centre for Drug Statistics Methodology. ATC classification
  index with DDDs 2026. Oslo, Norway.
  https://atcddd.fhi.no/


U.S. National Library of Medicine: RxNorm and DailyMed
------------------------------------------------------
Open Altio gives each ingredient its code from RxNorm's Current Prescribable
Content, and the label flags read FDA's human prescription labels as DailyMed
publishes them, with DailyMed's file linking each label to RxNorm. All of them
come from the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM), and none is included in
this repository: `python -m open_altio.faers labels-fetch` downloads them from
NLM, `python -m open_altio.faers labels-refresh` fetches DailyMed's daily
update files, and the build reads RxNorm from NLM's files in a folder you name
(rules/label-source.md, rules/rxnorm-ingredients.md). The reviewed name pairs
in mappings/faers/rxnorm/ write each substance as RxNorm names it.

This product uses publicly available data from the U.S. National Library of
Medicine (NLM), National Institutes of Health, Department of Health and Human
Services; NLM is not responsible for the product and does not endorse or
recommend this or any other product.

- RxNorm: https://www.nlm.nih.gov/research/umls/rxnorm/
- DailyMed: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/


EMA Important Medical Events
----------------------------
When you give its file for the release's MedDRA version, the label flags read
the European Medicines Agency's Important Medical Events (IME) list: a reaction
on it is a serious form, which a label that names only the reaction's group
does not cover (rules/label-umbrella.md). The list is EMA's, published free for
each MedDRA version. It is not included in this repository and is read from a
file you name, like MedDRA's own files.


MedDRA
------
MedDRA(R) is a registered trademark of ICH. No MedDRA content is included in
this repository. The local explorer uses MedDRA only from files you point it
at, under your own MedDRA subscription. The hosted service at
open.altioai.com shows MedDRA's hierarchy and SMQs only to people whose
subscription it has verified with MSSO.
